An ant's purpose, like that of any living thing, is not assigned but emergent: it survives, reproduces, and sustains the colony that gives its life meaning and function. Individual ants serve specialized roles—foraging, defending, tending brood—so the colony acts as a kind of superorganism pursuing its own continuation. In a broader sense, ants also serve ecological purposes: aerating soil, dispersing seeds, recycling nutrients, and feeding countless other species. Whether any of this constitutes a "purpose" depends on whether you see purpose as built into nature or as a story we tell about it.
